oris-trace 1
# Equal-size free blocks share one red-black tree node as a chain. Four 400-byte blocks,
# kept apart by live 300-byte blocks so they cannot coalesce, are freed in turn: the first
# to reach the tree becomes a node, the next two join its chain (tree+ chain). Five
# distinct larger sizes then build a tree deep enough to rotate. Freeing the separator
# between the chain head and a chain member coalesces all three: the member leaves the
# chain (tree- link) and the head's successor is promoted in its place (tree- promote).
# A 1100-byte request then takes the merged block (the most-recently-freed one), so the
# next 400-byte request searches the tree: it takes a chain member, not the head,
# because unlinking a chain member needs no rebalancing (tree- link).
alloc h0 400
alloc h1 300
alloc h2 400
alloc h3 300
alloc h4 400
alloc h5 300
alloc h6 400
alloc h7 300
alloc h8 600
alloc h9 300
alloc h10 800
alloc h11 300
alloc h12 1000
alloc h13 300
alloc h14 1200
alloc h15 300
alloc h16 1400
alloc h17 300
free h0
free h2
free h4
free h6
free h8
free h10
free h12
free h14
free h16
free h1
alloc h18 1100
alloc h19 400
purge
